geoipupdate for Debian
----------------------

In order to use this package, edit /etc/GeoIP.conf or pass another
configuration file to geoipupdate using the -f flag. You need a MaxMind
account ID and license key; both are available from
https://www.maxmind.com/en/accounts/current/license-key. The scheduled runs
described below always read /etc/GeoIP.conf; -f applies to manual runs only.

The update runs daily at a randomized time. Under systemd, geoipupdate.timer
spreads it across 24 hours, at an offset fixed per machine. Otherwise
/etc/cron.d/geoipupdate spreads it across the 12 hours after 00:47, picking a
fresh time on every run. Both stay inert until /etc/GeoIP.conf holds a real
account ID; neither checks LicenseKey, so a config with only one of the two
will run and fail to authenticate.

After editing the config, run "systemctl start geoipupdate.service" to check
your credentials rather than waiting up to a day for the timer. "systemctl
status geoipupdate.service" and "journalctl -u geoipupdate.service" show
whether the scheduled updates are working.

The databases are written to /usr/share/GeoIP. The systemd service runs in a
sandbox that permits writes only there, so if you change DatabaseDirectory or
LockFile, add the new path with "systemctl edit geoipupdate.service" and a
ReadWritePaths= line. Otherwise timer-driven runs fail with "read-only file
system" even though manual runs as root succeed.
